    <text x="28" y="221" font-size="12" fill="#a9bec5">No tube-sheet contact, welds, plasticity, buckling, fatigue, or mapped CFD temperature.</text></g>
  </svg>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Assumed value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Inner / outer radius</td><td>10 / 11 mm</td><td>Uniform circular wall</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Length</td><td>1 m</td><td>Pressure reference away from end effects</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pressure</td><td>2 MPa inside; 0 outside</td><td>Gauge pressures; pressure case only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>E / ν</td><td>190 GPa / 0.30</td><td>Assumed constant isotropic elasticity</td></tr>
    <tr><td>α / ΔT</td><td>16 × 10⁻⁶ K⁻¹ / 80 K</td><td>Uniform thermal-only cases</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>No actual alloy strength, temperature-dependent properties, fatigue limit, or allowable stress has been supplied. Consequently <strong>hardware safety is NOT_ESTABLISHED</strong>, even when the equation checks pass.</p>
  <h2 id="pressure">1. Pressure proof: equilibrium before a contour plot.</h2>
  <p>Tension is positive. A long, rotationally symmetric elastic cylinder must satisfy radial equilibrium. The Lamé solution simultaneously satisfies that equation and the inner/outer pressure tractions.</p>
  <ProofEquation label="Compatibility: radial displacement defines strain; isotropic elasticity and equilibrium fix its shape" latex={String.raw`\epsilon_r=u_r',\quad \epsilon_\theta=\frac{u_r}{r},\quad \epsilon_z=\mathrm{const},\qquad u_r''+\frac{u_r'}{r}-\frac{u_r}{r^2}=0\Rightarrow u_r=C_1r+\frac{C_2}{r}`} />
  <ProofEquation label="Radial equilibrium and Lamé stress field" latex={String.raw`\frac{d\sigma_r}{dr}+\frac{\sigma_r-\sigma_\theta}{r}=0,\qquad \sigma_r=A-\frac{B}{r^2},\quad\sigma_\theta=A+\frac{B}{r^2}`} />
  <ProofEquation label="Constants from the two pressure boundary conditions" latex={String.raw`A=\frac{p_i a^2-p_o b^2}{b^2-a^2},\qquad B=\frac{a^2b^2(p_i-p_o)}{b^2-a^2}`} />
  <p>The check is explicit: differentiation gives 2B/r³; the hoop-minus-radial term supplies −2B/r³. Their sum is zero. At r = a, radial stress is −pᵢ; at r = b, it is −pₒ. The script tests both tractions rather than accepting a plausible-looking stress contour.</p>
  <ProofEquation label="Closed-end force balance—not open ends, and not plane strain" latex={String.raw`\sigma_z\pi(b^2-a^2)=\pi(p_i a^2-p_o b^2),\qquad \sigma_z=A`} />
  <ProofEquation label="Radial displacement from isotropic Hooke's law" latex={String.raw`u_r(r)=\frac{(1-2\nu)Ar+(1+\nu)B/r}{E},\qquad \epsilon_z=\frac{(1-2\nu)A}{E}`} />
  <table><thead><tr><th>Calculated reference</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Bore radial stress</td><td>{(inner.radial_Pa/1e6).toFixed(3)} MPa</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bore hoop stress</td><td>{(inner.hoop_Pa/1e6).toFixed(6)} MPa</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Closed-end axial stress</td><td>{(inner.axial_Pa/1e6).toFixed(6)} MPa</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bore von Mises stress</td><td>{(inner.von_mises_Pa/1e6).toFixed(6)} MPa</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Outer radial displacement</td><td>{(outer.radial_displacement_m*1e6).toFixed(6)} μm</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>The thin-wall estimate pᵢa/t gives 20 MPa, versus 21.048 MPa at the bore in the thick-wall calculation. Geometry determines whether that approximation is adequate. These stresses are not applicable at ports, tube-sheet joints, welds, or end-cap transitions.</p>
  <h2 id="thermal">2. Thermal proof: the boundary condition is the physics.</h2>
  <p>For the thermal-only axial model, the transverse surfaces are traction-free. Axial elastic strain is total axial strain minus thermal strain. A sliding end and two axially fixed ends solve different engineering problems.</p>
  <ProofEquation label="Axial thermoelastic constitutive equation" latex={String.raw`\sigma_z=E\left(\frac{du}{dx}-\alpha\Delta T\right)`} />
  <ProofEquation label="Case A: one datum, other end free; uniform heating creates no axial stress" latex={String.raw`\sigma_z=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad u(L)=\alpha\Delta T L=1.280\ \mathrm{mm}`} />
  <ProofEquation label="Case B: both ends axially fixed; transverse contraction remains free" latex={String.raw`u(0)=u(L)=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad \sigma_z=-E\alpha\Delta T=-243.200\ \mathrm{MPa}`} />
  <ProofEquation label="Reaction force independently closes the constrained bar" latex={String.raw`S=\pi(b^2-a^2),\qquad |R|=S E\alpha\Delta T=16.045\ \mathrm{kN}`} />
  <p>Negative stress means compression. It does not mean the real tube can sustain it: yielding, buckling, support compliance, gradients, and tube-sheet behavior are omitted. Nor is −EαΔT the formula for fully triaxial restraint. As <a href="https://www.comsol.com/blogs/how-to-provide-structural-stability-in-thermal-expansion-simulations">COMSOL's constraint example explains</a>, adding restraints can produce a converged but physically irrelevant result.</p>
  <h2 id="verification">3. Actual small FE calculations, compared with the derivations.</h2>
  <p>The downloadable Python implementation assembles and solves stiffness matrices. This is an educational radial FE / axial-bar calculation, <strong>not an ANSYS or CalculiX run</strong>. It does not replace Aero's existing conventional-solver qualification infrastructure.</p>
  <ProofEquation label="Radial FE: displacement interpolation plus a constant axial-strain degree of freedom" latex={String.raw`\mathbf K=\sum_e\int_e\mathbf B^T\mathbf D\mathbf B\,2\pi rL\,dr,\qquad\mathbf K\mathbf q=\mathbf f`} />
  <p>The pressure model uses the full isotropic radial/hoop/axial constitutive matrix, three-point Gauss integration, and the closed-end pressure force. Linear radial elements are refined through five meshes. Stress error is evaluated at quadrature points against the independent analytical field—not against the next mesh alone.</p>
  <img src={`${assets}/pressure-convergence.svg`} width="680" height="375" alt="Actual pressure finite-element stress error decreases as radial meshes refine from 4 to 64 elements." loading="lazy" />
  <table><thead><tr><th>Radial elements</th><th>Outer displacement error</th><th>Stress relative L₂ error</th><th>Equilibrium residual</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    {results.pressure_fe.map(row => <tr><td>{row.elements}</td><td>{(row.outer_u_relative_error*100).toFixed(6)}%</td><td>{(row.gauss_stress_relative_L2_error*100).toFixed(4)}%</td><td>{row.normalized_equilibrium_residual.toExponential(2)}</td></tr>)}
  </tbody></table>
  <ProofEquation label="Thermal bar: element stiffness and equivalent thermal load" latex={String.raw`\mathbf k_e=\frac{ES}{\ell}\begin{bmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{bmatrix},\qquad\mathbf f_{th,e}=ES\alpha\Delta T\begin{bmatrix}-1\\1\end{bmatrix}`} />
  <p>Both thermal cases were solved with 2, 4, 8, and 16 elements. The linear free-expansion field and zero-displacement restrained field are represented exactly by these elements; stresses and reactions agree to floating-point precision. That exactness is specific to this simple problem, not proof that a real exchanger is mesh-independent.</p>
  <div class="scope-notice"><strong>{results.tests.run} executable tests passed · {results.tests.failures} failures · {results.tests.errors} errors</strong><p>Boundary tractions, end-force balance, differential equilibrium, zero-load limit, pressure refinement, free expansion, fixed-end reactions, units/known values, and rejection of invalid geometry. Finest pressure stress error: {(finest.gauss_stress_relative_L2_error*100).toFixed(4)}%. Physical validation and hardware qualification remain NOT_ESTABLISHED.</p></div>
  <h2 id="explore">Try the first-principles equations.</h2>
  <p>Change synthetic inputs locally in your browser. This recalculates the <strong>closed-form equations only</strong>; it does not run FEA or change the frozen verification record above. No prompt, geometry, or input is sent to a server.</p>
  <form id="hx-proof-form" class="input-grid">
